From: Abhishek Vyas <abhishek.vyas@inventum.net>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: nss: deliver EAPOL to the nl80211 control port

With nss_offload=1 the WPA2 four-way handshake never completes. The
station answers M1 with M2, hostapd never advances to M3, retransmits M1
four times and deauthenticates after about 8 s. Reproduced on both bands
on udaya_a6-id2 (IPQ5018 + QCN6122); it clears immediately on returning
to nss_offload=0.

M2 does reach the host - tcpdump on the wlanN netdev shows every
retransmission - but hostapd never sees it. hostapd runs driver=nl80211
and the driver advertises CONTROL_PORT_OVER_NL80211, so hostapd receives
EAPOL through cfg80211_rx_control_port() rather than from a packet socket
on the netdev. ath11k_nss_deliver_rx() hands NSS frames straight to
napi_gro_receive(), bypassing the mac80211 RX handlers where that control
port branch lives, so EAPOL is delivered as ordinary data and the
authenticator is never fed. The function already carries a TODO noting it
should move into mac80211.

ath11k_mac_op_start() is supposed to prevent this by asking firmware to
route EAPOL to the WBM release ring via the CCE, keeping it off the NSS
path. That WMI call succeeds on IPQ5018 - neither of its failure paths
logs anything - but the frames still arrive through NSS, so the rule is
evidently not honoured on this chipset.

Pass EAPOL to the control port in addition to the existing delivery.
cfg80211_rx_control_port() copies the skb, matching how mac80211 calls it
in ieee80211_deliver_skb_to_local_stack(), so the frame can still go up
the netdev afterwards and napi_gro_receive() continues to own it. Doing
this additively rather than diverting keeps any setup that reads EAPOL
from a packet socket working, since the driver cannot see whether
userspace asked for the control port - control_port_over_nl80211 lives in
the private ieee80211_sub_if_data, not in ieee80211_vif.

Fixing this inside ath11k_nss_deliver_rx() covers every caller: the
normal vdev path, the AP_VLAN ext_vdev path, mesh and the WDS 4-addr
exception paths all funnel through it.

Two known limitations, both wanting review before this is considered
final:

- The unencrypted argument is hardcoded false. mac80211 derives it from
  RX_FLAG_DECRYPTED, which the NSS path does not carry. NSS decrypts in
  hardware, so false is right for every EAPOL except the plaintext M2.
  Reporting a plaintext frame as protected weakens the signal userspace
  uses to reject unencrypted EAPOL.
- EAPOL continues up the netdev as it does today, so it still reaches the
  bridge. mac80211 deliberately avoids that (see
  313-mac80211-do-not-accept-forward-invalid-EAPOL-frames.patch). A
  production fix should consult the vif's control port state and stop
  forwarding when the control port owns the frame.

Signed-off-by: Abhishek Vyas <abhishek.vyas@inventum.net>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
@@ -503,6 +503,8 @@ static int ath11k_nss_deliver_rx(struct

 deliver_msdu:
 	skb->protocol = eth_type_trans(skb, dev);
+	if (unlikely(skb->protocol == cpu_to_be16(ETH_P_PAE)))
+		cfg80211_rx_control_port(dev, skb, false);
 	napi_gro_receive(napi, skb);
 	return 0;

@@ -524,6 +526,8 @@ deliver_amsdu:
 	while (!skb_queue_empty(&subframe_list)) {
 		subframe = __skb_dequeue(&subframe_list);
 		subframe->protocol = eth_type_trans(subframe, dev);
+		if (unlikely(subframe->protocol == cpu_to_be16(ETH_P_PAE)))
+			cfg80211_rx_control_port(dev, subframe, false);
 		napi_gro_receive(napi, subframe);
 	}

